Step-by-Step Flow

1. Client Calls AgentCore
The client (Jupyter notebook) invoked a tool through the AgentCore Gateway using:

mcp("tools/call", ...)

The request included a token passed as:
"user_token"

This token represents the identity of the caller.


2. AgentCore Gateway Receives the Request
The AgentCore Gateway processes the request and triggers a Request Interceptor Lambda before the tool is executed.

The interceptor acts as a security control point where authentication or token transformation can occur.


3. Interceptor Performs Token Exchange
Inside the interceptor Lambda, the code calls the Auth0 OAuth endpoint:

POST https://{AUTH0_DOMAIN}/oauth/token

using the OAuth Token Exchange grant type:

grant_type = urn:ietf:params:oauth:grant-type:token-exchange

The interceptor sends the following parameters:

subject_token       = original incoming token
subject_token_type  = urn:fastmcp:mcp
audience            = https://api.addition.example.com
scope               = calc:add
client_id           = AgentCore Test Application client
client_secret       = matching client secret

This tells Auth0:

"Exchange this incoming token for a new token that only has permission to call the Addition API."


4. Auth0 Issues a Scoped Token
Auth0 validates the request and returns a new access token.

This new token contains reduced permissions and a different target audience.

The token is now scoped specifically for the downstream API.


5. Gateway Continues Tool Execution
After the token exchange completes successfully, the gateway continues processing the request.

The downstream tool executes:

addNumbers(5,3)

and returns the expected result.


Verification of Downscoping

We decoded both the original token and the exchanged token to confirm the exchange worked correctly.

Original Token Claims
aud: https://agent-core-test-api
scope: read:test invoke:runtime

This token was intended for AgentCore runtime access.

Exchanged Token Claims
aud: https://api.addition.example.com
scope: calc:add

This confirms the exchanged token was:

1. Targeted to the downstream Addition API
2. Reduced to only the calc:add permission

This verifies that the token was successfully downscoped.


What Was Successfully Demonstrated

1. AgentCore Gateway can forward identity tokens to an interceptor.

2. The interceptor Lambda can perform OAuth token exchange with Auth0.

3. Auth0 can issue a new token with restricted audience and scope.

4. The downstream tool can execute successfully using the exchanged token.


Security Benefit

This pattern implements least-privilege access.

Instead of giving downstream tools a full user token, the system exchanges the token and creates a limited token specifically for the target API.

